message: "If you cite this computational and health-systems research thesis, please use the metadata below."
type: thesis
title: "Closed-Loop Agentic Care Planning for Cervical Cancer: A Gated Action-Observation Framework, Synthetic Worked Example and Study Protocol"
authors:
  - family-names: Ogbonna
    given-names: Kelechi Emeka
    email: kelechiogbonna300@gmail.com
    alias: cloudynirvana
    # Add your ORCID iD once registered at https://orcid.org, in this form:
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
abstract: >-
  A pathology report states what a tumour is, not what should happen next. This thesis treats
  the care of one patient as a partially observed control problem: the state is hidden, actions
  either gather information or change the state, and decision gates stop a plan from committing
  to a therapeutic class until the observations that decide it are present. An agent maintains
  the loop by structuring reports, holding the observation record, ranking unknown observations
  by the pathway branches they switch, mapping observations to guideline options with their
  evidence, matching trials, and raising safety and timing alerts. Clinicians stage, choose,
  dose and consent. The framework is specified as a disease-independent case schema, a rule set
  per pathology, an evidence store and a five-phase loop, and is demonstrated on one invented
  cervical cancer case in which four misreadings are seeded as test items. Seventeen agent
  capabilities are named across six layers. Four evaluation studies are proposed with stated
  hypotheses and sample sizes: retrospective concordance with tumour-board decisions, process
  timing, cross-pathology reuse, and an exploratory late-stage palliative pathway. None has been
  run. Protocol stage, no outcome data. Research only. Not a medical device, not clinical
  decision support in use, not a dose, not a cure.
keywords:
  - agentic medicine
  - closed-loop care
  - partially observable decision process
  - value of information
  - decision gates
  - tumour board
  - cervical cancer
  - low-resource oncology
  - clinical workflow
  - study protocol
  - research-only
